<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hypatia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 355–415</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hypatia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> philosopher, mathematician and astronomer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, the daughter of the mathematician Theon. She taught philosophy to pagan and Christian pupils and advised the city's leaders. In March 415 she was murdered by a Christian mob during a conflict between the prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Cyril, and she later became a symbol of learning destroyed by intolerance.</p></div>
